From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 01:45:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09386/11362] Teach the QA-layer audit two bug classes it could
 not have found

Tonight's audit of exp6478 turned up two defects. Neither is covered by the five
bug classes the adversarial audit asks about, so the audit could have reviewed
both files and correctly reported CLEAN. Adding them to BUG_CLASS_MARKERS and to
all three prompts; the existing coverage test enforces that every prompt asks
every class.

F / 8b. DEFAULT BRANCH DISABLES THE CHECK. A recognizer chain ending in a
default that means NO CHECK rather than "unrecognized, say so". The tell is a
terminal `return None` whose caller reads it as permission to skip, which
inverts the safe direction: an input the chain was never taught reads as
approved rather than unverified. This is not a hypothetical class --
`duration_floor_for_artifact` did exactly this, 720 artifacts declared a
substrate and got no duration floor, 237 of them ran under a second, and the
same shape had ALREADY been patched for a single substrate on 2026-07-30. A
one-case patch of a general defect is why the class is needed.

G / 8c. METRIC COMPUTED BEFORE THE WORK IT MEASURES. A duration or counter
evaluated before the operation it describes -- commonest as a value computed
inline as a CALL ARGUMENT, since arguments evaluate before the callee runs. The
result is a constant wearing a measurement's name, and every downstream check
trusting it is inert. Four experiment scripts shipped this in one milestone.

Both entries carry the measured precedent rather than an abstract description,
matching the existing classes' style: a reviewer given a worked case recognises
the shape, and the numbers make clear this is a live defect and not a style
preference.

Verified by mutation: removing one class from PER_GUARD_PROMPT fails
test_every_prompt_covers_every_bug_class by name. Restore confirmed
byte-identical. 79 tests pass.

test_the_marker_list_covers_all_five_classes is renamed and updated 5 -> 7. The
exact-count assertion is kept deliberately so the next person to add a class has
to come here and say so.

